<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puno Region</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Department of Puno</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puno">Puno</a> Region is a region of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, covering the northern and western shores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> and the high Altiplano. It was home to Pukará and later to the Colla and Lupaca kingdoms, and as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Puno it joined the South Peruvian Republic in 1836. Its capital is the city of Puno.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/puno-region.jpg" alt="Puno Region" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Incacity · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isla_Suasi_PUNO-PERU.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puno">Puno</a> lies in the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and is the fifth largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the country, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>, Madre de Dios, Ucayali and Loreto. It is bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> to the east and by the departments of Madre de Dios, Cusco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arequipa">Arequipa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moquegua">Moquegua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a>. Its capital, the city of Puno, stands on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> in the Altiplano, or high sierra, on the Collao Plateau. The Andean mountains make up 70 per cent of the territory and the rest is covered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon rainforest</a>.</p><p>The western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a>, the world's highest navigable lake, lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. Its islands include Taquile, Suasi and Amantaní, known for weaving and pre-Columbian constructions. The Uros live on floating islands made of totora reeds and travel in boats of the same material. The Titicaca National Reserve protects extensive stretches of reeds and many species. The climate is cold and dry with a four-month rainy season, while the rainforest is warm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In ancient times the Collao plateau was inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a> groups, among them the Collas, Zapanas, Kallahuayos and Lupacas, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechuas</a> came later. The area was the territory of the Tiahuanacos, between 800 and 1200 CE, who were the highest cultural expression of the Aymara people in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> took over these lands in the fifteenth century. The chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-garcilaso-de-la-vega">Inca Garcilaso de la Vega</a> places the legend of Manco Cápac and Mama Ocllo, who emerged from the waters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> to found the Inca Empire, in these ancient times.</p><p>Under Spanish rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puno">Puno</a> was the obligatory route for travellers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, and the Spanish, drawn by mining, left an important colonial legacy. In 1668 the viceroy, the Conde de Lemos, established San Juan Bautista de Puno as capital of the province of Paucarcolla; it was later called San Carlos de Puno after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a> and became capital of the Intendancy of Puno. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> was created by the Reglamento Provisional de Elecciones of 26 April 1822. In 1870 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arequipa">Arequipa</a> to Puno railway was installed and navigation began on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> is divided into 13 provinces and 107 districts, and is run by a regional government. Indigenous people are the majority. In the 2007 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> the language first learnt by most residents was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> (38.01 per cent), followed by Spanish (34.81 per cent) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a> (26.93 per cent). The Quechua spoken is the Cusco-Collao variety.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Department of Puno” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pukará grew from about 500 BCE on the northern side of the Titicaca basin in <b>Puno</b>, Peru.</blockquote><a href="/state/pukara">Pukará</a></li><li><blockquote>After Andrés de Santa Cruz's Bolivian army defeated his Peruvian opponents, an assembly of the southern departments of Peru, Arequipa, Cusco, <b>Puno</b> and Ayacucho, met at Sicuani in March 1836 and declared the South Peruvian Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/south-peruvian-republic">South Peruvian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
